---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'What does Mbps mean?',
    answer:
      'Mbps means megabits per second, the unit internet plans use for speed. MB/s means megabytes per second, a unit a download manager may show. Eight bits make one byte, like eight slices make a pizza.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How fast is a 100 Mbps connection in MB/s?',
    answer:
      'About 12.5 MB/s, because eight bits fit in one byte.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much internet speed does a household need?',
    answer:
      'It depends on what everyone does at once. A 4K stream calls for about 15 to 20 Mbps down, two 1080p streams about 5 Mbps each, and a Zoom 1080p group call about 3.8 Mbps down and 3 Mbps up. That mix uses about 34 Mbps down before headroom. The FCC fixed broadband benchmark is 100 Mbps down and 20 Mbps up.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does upload speed matter?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Upload speed helps with video calls, live streaming, cloud backups, and sending big files. Most plans have less upload because homes historically used the internet mostly to download, so cable networks give most capacity to the download direction.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good ping for games and calls?',
    answer:
      'There is no single target for every game. Zoom says latency of 150 ms or less is typically recommended for calls, while lower ping can make interactive play feel more responsive. The route to a particular game server matters too.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I make a better speed guess?',
    answer:
      'Play speed detective: check your plan, count who else may be streaming, notice whether you are using a phone or laptop, and think about which room you are in. Use those clues to make your download and upload guesses.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Internet Speed 101 | Guess the Speed"
  description="A friendly guide to Mbps, download and upload speed, ping, Wi-Fi, and how much internet a home may need."
  breadcrumb="Internet speed 101"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}></script>
  </Fragment>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <span class="round-banner">Speed school</span>
    <h1>Internet speed 101</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Speed labels stop looking like secret code once you know a few simple ideas. Let’s turn
      bits, bytes, ping, and Wi-Fi into friendly clues for your next Guess the Speed round.
    </p>
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>Bits, bytes, and the numbers on a plan</h2>
      <p>
        Eight bits make one byte. Picture a byte as a pizza cut into eight slices: each slice
        stands for one bit, and all eight slices make the byte. Internet plans use megabits per
        second (Mbps), while a download manager may show megabytes per second (MB/s). Divide Mbps
        by 8 to estimate MB/s, so 100 Mbps is about 12.5 MB/s.
      </p>
      <p>
        You may also see kbps, Mbps, or Gbps. The prefixes tell you the scale: in decimal units,
        1 Mbps is 1,000 kbps and 1 Gbps is 1,000 Mbps.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Internet speed and estimated five gigabyte download times" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Approximate speed conversion and time for a 5 GB game</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Internet speed</th>
              <th scope="col">About this many MB/s</th>
              <th scope="col">Time for 5 GB</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">10 Mbps</th><td>1.25 MB/s</td><td>About 1 hr 7 min</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">25 Mbps</th><td>3.125 MB/s</td><td>About 27 min</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">50 Mbps</th><td>6.25 MB/s</td><td>About 13 min</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">100 Mbps</th><td>12.5 MB/s</td><td>About 7 min</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">300 Mbps</th><td>37.5 MB/s</td><td>About 2 min</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">500 Mbps</th><td>62.5 MB/s</td><td>About 1 min 20 sec</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">1,000 Mbps</th><td>125 MB/s</td><td>About 40 sec</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-table-note">
        The estimate uses decimal units (1 GB = 8,000 megabits) and exactly 5 × 8,000 / Mbps
        seconds, rounded to the nearest minute, or to seconds for fast lines. Real downloads can
        take longer.
      </p>

      <h2>Bandwidth and latency: lanes versus trip time</h2>
      <p>
        Picture a highway carrying data. Bandwidth is like the number of lanes: it describes how
        much room there is for traffic. Latency is like the time one trip takes from start to
        finish. A speed test reports both download speed and ping because a wide road and a quick
        trip are different clues.
      </p>

      <h2>Download versus upload</h2>
      <p>
        Download is data coming to your device. Upload is data going from your device to the
        internet. Most home plans are asymmetric, meaning the two directions are not equal.
        Cable plans may have less upload capacity, while some fiber plans offer similar speeds in
        both directions. Check both numbers when you compare plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        Upload is not an afterthought. It matters for video calls, live streaming, cloud backups,
        and sending big files. Each of those asks your device to send data out, not just bring it
        in.
      </p>

      <h2>Ping and jitter</h2>
      <p>
        Ping, also called latency, is the round-trip time for a small request, measured in
        milliseconds (ms). Lower ping means a quicker response. Jitter is the average change
        between consecutive ping measurements, so it helps describe how steady those times are.
        For games and calls, a quick response can matter more than a huge download number.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Zoom latency and jitter recommendations" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Zoom's typical network recommendations</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">Typical recommendation</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Latency</th><td>150 ms or less</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Jitter</th><td>40 ms or less</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-table-note">
        <a href="https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&amp;sysparm_article=KB0070504">Zoom's network-statistics guide</a>{' '}
        calls these typical recommendations. Games do not share one universal ping threshold, so
        check the route and server for the game you play.
      </p>

      <h2>How much speed do you need?</h2>
      <p>
        Add the things your household does at the same time, then leave room for busy moments.
        Netflix recommends at least 15 Mbps for 4K; YouTube recommends a sustained 20 Mbps, so
        think about 15 to 20 Mbps per 4K stream rather than one universal figure. Two HD streams
        at about 5 Mbps each and one Zoom 1080p group call at about 3.8 Mbps down and 3 Mbps up
        bring that example to roughly 29 to 34 Mbps down before headroom.{' '}
        <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/document/fcc-increases-broadband-speed-benchmark">The FCC's 2024 fixed broadband benchmark is 100 Mbps down and 20 Mbps up</a>.
        {' '}It is a benchmark, not a guarantee that every home needs that exact plan.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Approximate internet speed needs by activity" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Approximate speed per stream or device</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Download and upload</th><th scope="col">A quick note</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Email and browsing</th><td>No fixed per-device rate</td><td>Page size and activity vary</td></tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HD video</th>
              <td>About 5 Mbps down</td>
              <td><a href="https://help.netflix.com/en/node/306">Netflix 1080p</a> and <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/78358">YouTube 1080p</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">4K video</th>
              <td>About 15 to 20 Mbps down</td>
              <td><a href="https://help.netflix.com/en/node/306">Netflix UHD</a> and <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/78358">YouTube 4K</a> recommendations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HD group video call</th>
              <td>About 2.6 down / 1.8 up at 720p; 3.8 down / 3.0 up at 1080p</td>
              <td><a href="https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&amp;sysparm_article=KB0060748">Zoom's published requirements</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">PlayStation Remote Play</th>
              <td>5 Mbps each way minimum; 15 Mbps each way recommended</td>
              <td><a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/support/games/ps4-remote-play-connection-troubleshooting/">Remote Play guidance</a>; regular games vary</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="learn-more">
        For household starting ranges and planning advice, read{' '}
        <a href="/what-is-a-good-internet-speed/">What is a good internet speed?</a>.
        Add overlapping streams, calls, and devices with the{' '}
        <a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">household speed calculator</a>.
        To convert Mbps to MB/s and estimate a download time, use the{' '}
        <a href="/mbps-to-mbs/">Mbps to MB/s converter</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Wi-Fi versus a cable</h2>
      <p>
        Wi-Fi speed can change with distance, walls, older devices, and neighbours' networks.
        The 2.4 GHz band reaches farther, but it is slower and more crowded. The 5 GHz and 6 GHz
        bands are faster over shorter distances. A cable usually gives a clearer look at the real
        speed your plan can deliver.
      </p>

      <h2>Tips for a better guess</h2>
      <p>
        Get your clues before game night’s speed test. Think like a friendly speed detective:
        gather what you know, make a smart estimate, then see how close the family gets.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Check the speed listed on your internet plan for your first clue.</li>
        <li>Count who else might be streaming at the same time.</li>
        <li>Notice whether you are testing on a phone or a laptop.</li>
        <li>Think about the room you are in and how strong the Wi-Fi feels there.</li>
        <li>Make separate download and upload guesses, then lock them in before the reveal.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>A tiny internet glossary</h2>
      <dl class="guide-glossary">
        <dt>Bandwidth</dt>
        <dd>The capacity of a connection to carry data.</dd>
        <dt>Throughput</dt>
        <dd>The speed of data that actually gets through.</dd>
        <dt>Latency</dt>
        <dd>The time a small request takes to make a round trip, often shown as ping.</dd>
        <dt>Jitter</dt>
        <dd>How much the time between pings changes from one measurement to the next.</dd>
        <dt>ISP</dt>
        <dd>Your internet service provider, the company that connects your home.</dd>
        <dt>Router</dt>
        <dd>The device that shares your home internet connection with your devices.</dd>
        <dt>Mesh</dt>
        <dd>A group of Wi-Fi points that work together to cover more of a home.</dd>
        <dt>Fiber</dt>
        <dd>An internet connection that carries data over fiber-optic cables.</dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
